-- BIB 101 unit 1, part 2 of 6

do $seed$
declare cid uuid;
begin
  select id into cid from public.courses where slug = 'bib-101';
  insert into public.course_items (course_id, kind, position, title, content) select cid, 'topic', 2, $j$1.2 Promise: Abraham and the Covenant$j$, $j${"readings": [], "body": "Read Genesis 12, 15 and 22 and Genesis 50:15 to 21 in the King James Version. Then read this lesson.\n\nAfter the fall, sin spread, and God dealt with the world through the flood and then through the scattering at Babel. In Genesis 12 God turned to one man. He called Abram to leave his country and promised to make of him a great nation, to bless him, and to bless all the families of the earth through him. God later changed his name to Abraham. His faith is summarized in Genesis 15:6, and his greatest test came on Mount Moriah in Genesis 22.\n\nThe promise passed to his son Isaac, then to Jacob, whom God renamed Israel. Jacob had twelve sons. One of them, Joseph, was sold into Egypt by his brothers, rose to rule under Pharaoh, and later saved his family from famine. Genesis ends with Israel living in Egypt.\n\nKey words: covenant, promise, faith, righteousness, tribe.\n\nYour task: (1) List the three parts of God's promise to Abram in Genesis 12:1 to 3. (2) Say in your own words what Genesis 15:6 teaches about faith. (3) Explain what Joseph meant in Genesis 50:20.\n\nCourse area: Bible elective. Scripture is read from the King James Version.", "video": "", "presenter": "Dr. Alex L. Jenkins Sr.", "standards": "Bible elective", "script": "Last lesson we saw sin enter the world. Today we see how God began to answer it, and the answer starts with one man and one promise.\n\nAfter the fall, things got worse. By Genesis chapter 6, the earth was filled with violence, and God sent the flood. After the flood, people gathered at Babel and tried to build their own way to heaven, and God scattered them. Then, in Genesis chapter 12, God did something new. He spoke to one man named Abram.\n\nScripture says, Now the LORD had said unto Abram, Get thee out of thy country, and from thy kindred, and from thy father's house, unto a land that I will shew thee: And I will make of thee a great nation, and I will bless thee, and make thy name great; and thou shalt be a blessing: And I will bless them that bless thee, and curse him that curseth thee: and in thee shall all families of the earth be blessed. Listen for three promises there. A land. A great nation. And a blessing that would reach every family on earth.\n\nAbram did not know the way, but he went. Years later, with no child in his tent, he believed God's promise of descendants like the stars. Genesis 15:6 says, And he believed in the LORD; and he counted it to him for righteousness. That verse is one of the most important in the Old Testament. It tells us that trusting God is the foundation of a right relationship with Him.\n\nGod changed his name to Abraham and gave him a son, Isaac, in his old age. In Genesis 22, God tested Abraham on Mount Moriah, and Abraham showed that he trusted God above everything he loved. The promise then passed from Isaac to Jacob, whom God renamed Israel, and Jacob had twelve sons.\n\nOne of those sons was Joseph. His brothers sold him into Egypt, yet God raised him up to save the family from famine. Years later he told those same brothers, in Genesis 50:20, But as for you, ye thought evil against me; but God meant it unto good, to bring to pass, as it is this day, to save much people alive.\n\nThat is the lesson. People may mean evil, but God is working out His promise. Your task is to list the three parts of the promise, explain Genesis 15:6, and explain what Joseph meant. Then take the four question check.\n\nFaith waits for God's timing. Excellence, Faith, Purpose. I will see you in the next lesson."}$j$::jsonb where not exists (select 1 from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'topic' and title = $j$1.2 Promise: Abraham and the Covenant$j$);
end
$seed$;
